    <!-- =========================
         HERO
    ========================== -->

    <section class="about-hero">

        <span class="about-eyebrow">
            THE PERSON BEHIND WANDERLOFT
        </span>

        <div class="about-photo">
            <img
                src="/images/IMAGE NEHA.png"
                alt="Neha Kumari"
            >
        </div>

        <h1>
            More than a project.<br>
            <em>A journey.</em>
        </h1>

        <p class="about-intro">
            Wanderloft started as an idea to build something around
            travel as college resume Project. Along the way, it became something much more —
            a reflection of my curiosity, learning, patience,
            mistakes, and the interest to keep building.
        </p>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         MY BACKGROUND
    ========================== -->

    <section class="about-section">

        <div class="section-number">
            01
            <span>MY BACKGROUND</span>
        </div>

        <div class="about-section-content">

            <h2>
                Learning by building,<br>
                growing through <em>challenges.</em>
            </h2>

            <p>
                My journey into software development started with
                learning the fundamentals — programming, problem
                solving, data structures, databases, and understanding
                how different pieces of technology work together.
            </p>

            <p>
                Over time, I became more interested in building
                things rather than only learning concepts. Small
                programs slowly turned into web applications,
                full-stack projects, APIs, databases, and eventually
                projects that felt like real products as surely i take help from different resources and use of ai.
            </p>

            <p>
                There were plenty of moments when things didn't work.
                Bugs, unexpected errors, broken features, confusing
                documentation, and countless attempts at fixing the
                same problem were all part of the process.
            </p>

            <p>
                But that's also where most of my learning happened.
                Every problem forced me to understand something a
                little deeper.
            </p>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         WANDERLOFT STORY
    ========================== -->

    <section class="about-wanderloft">

        <div class="wanderloft-card">

            <div class="wanderloft-mark">
                ✦
            </div>

            <span class="card-eyebrow">
                WHY I BUILT WANDERLOFT
            </span>

            <h2>
                I wanted to create something
                that felt <em>Close To My Heart.</em>
            </h2>

            <p>
                 I originally started Wanderloft as a project to strengthen
            my resume and challenge myself to build a complete
            full-stack application.
            </p>

              <p>
            But somewhere along the way, it stopped feeling like
            just another project on my resume. I started caring about
            how it looked, how it felt, how people would experience it,
            and what else I could build with it.
        </p>
            <p>
                That idea slowly grew into a platform where people
                could discover destinations, explore unique stays,
                share travel moments, and experience the world
                through other people's journeys.
            </p>

            <p>
            That's why I'm still working on it.
        </p>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         THE STRUGGLE
    ========================== -->

    <section class="about-section about-struggle">

        <div class="section-number">
            02
            <span>THE STRUGGLE</span>
        </div>

        <div class="about-section-content">

            <h2>
                Not every step<br>
                was <em>easy.</em>
            </h2>

            <p>
                Building Wanderloft wasn't a straight path from an
                idea to a finished product.
            </p>

            <p>
                There were features that broke after working perfectly,
                technologies that had to be replaced, deployment issues,
                database problems, UI changes, and moments when I had
                no idea why something wasn't working.
            </p>

            <p>
                Sometimes the solution was simple.
                Sometimes it took hours to find.
                And sometimes the best solution was to start again.
            </p>

            <p class="strong-line">
                But I kept coming back to the problem.
            </p>

            <p>
                That patience became one of the most valuable things
                this project taught me.
            </p>

        </div>

    </section>


 

